Draw menu and tooltip shadows through KWindowShadow

QtCurve gives the shadow tiles to the window manager in
qt6/style/shadowhelper.cpp. The old code took one route only. It put the
eight tiles in the _KDE_NET_WM_SHADOW property of the X window through
qtcX11ShadowInstall(). A native Wayland client makes no XCB connection, so
qtcX11Enabled() is false. installX11Shadows() returns at once and the style
installs nothing. Menus, the drop down list of a combo box, tooltips and
detached toolbars therefore have no shadow on a Wayland session.

KWindowShadow from KF6WindowSystem carries the same eight tiles and the
same four padding values to the compositor. Its X11 backend writes the
_KDE_NET_WM_SHADOW property. Its Wayland backend uses the
org_kde_kwin_shadow_manager protocol, in
/usr/lib/qt6/plugins/kf6/kwindowsystem/KF6WindowSystemKWaylandPlugin.so.
The qt6 style already links KF6::WindowSystem in qt6/style/CMakeLists.txt,
so the port adds no dependency. libKF6WindowSystem.so.6 was already in the
NEEDED list of /usr/lib/qt6/plugins/styles/qtcurve.so before the patch, and
the list does not change.

The art stays QtCurve art. qtcShadowCreate() in lib/utils/shadow.cpp still
draws the tiles, with the arguments that qtcX11ShadowInit() passes in
lib/utils/x11helpers.cpp: colours 0.4/0.4/0.4 and 0.2/0.2/0.2, radius 4.
The length of the gradient comes from qtcX11ShadowSize(), which reports the
shadowSize key of the configuration. The default is 30 pixels. The padding
is one pixel less than the gradient, as in qtcX11ShadowInit(). The style
makes fresh tiles when the size changes.

A size of zero gives a clear shadow and a padding of minus one pixel. The
old code wrote that padding to the X property, where it became 4294967295.
The new code installs no shadow for a size of zero.

qtcShadowCreate() writes the pixels in the QTC_PIXEL_QT byte order. On a
little endian machine that order is blue, green, red, alpha, which is the
memory layout of QImage::Format_ARGB32_Premultiplied. qtcFillShadowPixel()
also multiplies each colour channel by the alpha. The tiles therefore need
no conversion. qtcShadowCreate() writes the tiles in the order that KWin
wants, so each tile goes straight to the matching KWindowShadow setter.

qtcShadowCreate() is private to lib/utils. lib/utils builds with
-fvisibility=hidden, so the symbol does not leave libqtcurve-utils.so.2.
qt6/style/CMakeLists.txt therefore compiles lib/utils/shadow.cpp into the
plugin, beside qt6/common/common.cpp. The exported symbols of
libqtcurve-utils.so.2 do not change.

A probe that shows a QMenu with the patched style reports the same shadow
on both sessions. With the default configuration it finds 8 tiles, padding
29,29,29,29, and KWindowShadow::isCreated() true. The top tile is 1 by 34
pixels in QImage::Format_ARGB32_Premultiplied. The 4 pixels of that tile
next to the window are clear, and the first gradient pixel is 0xfe656565.
With shadowSize 10 the padding is 9,9,9,9 and the top tile is 1 by 14
pixels. With shadowSize 100 the padding is 99,99,99,99.

On X11 the menu window then carries _KDE_NET_WM_SHADOW(CARDINAL) with 8
pixmap ids and 29, 29, 29, 29. The old plugin writes the same padding for
the sizes 10, 30 and 100. A menu that opens after qtcX11SetShadowSize(60)
carries 8 new pixmap ids and 59, 59, 59, 59.

KWindowShadow is a KDE Frameworks class, so the new code sits behind
QTC_QT6_ENABLE_KDE. A build without KDE Frameworks 6 keeps the X property
code, under the new name. The helper keeps acceptWidget(), the register,
unregister and event filter interface, and the install on WinIdChange. The
whole process shares the eight tiles. A shadow is a child of its QWindow,
so the window frees it. The helper also frees a shadow when the widget
unregisters and when the helper itself goes away.

Drop this patch when QtCurve installs shadows through KWindowShadow
upstream.

